(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["finetune",{"c:when-to-fine-tune-versus-prompt-or-retrieve":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Prompting, retrieval and fine-tuning fix different problems. Retrieval fixes \u003cb>what the model knows\u003c/b>: fresh, private or changing facts. Fine-tuning fixes \u003cb>how the model behaves\u003c/b>: a format, a style, a narrow skill done consistently, or a job that only a big model with a long prompt could do, moved onto a small cheap one. Fine-tune last, when evals show that prompting has plateaued and the volume or the behaviour justifies the extra work.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Two different kinds of failure\u003c/h4>\u003cp>When an AI feature fails, it usually fails in one of two ways.\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>A knowledge gap.\u003c/b> The model does not have the facts: your refund policy, last week's price change, a customer's order history. No amount of training on examples of \u003ci>style\u003c/i> fixes this. You fix it with \u003cb>context\u003c/b>: retrieval, tools and better context assembly.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>A behaviour gap.\u003c/b> The model has what it needs but does the wrong thing with it: breaks the JSON schema one time in fifty, ignores your house rules for ticket categories, writes in the wrong tone, or needs a 4,000-token prompt with thirty examples before it behaves. You fix this first with better prompts and examples, and then, if that plateaus, with \u003cb>fine-tuning\u003c/b>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Real systems often need both, for example a fine-tuned model that also receives retrieved passages. Research backs the split. Ovadia et al. (2023) found retrieval consistently beat fine-tuning for injecting knowledge. Gekhman et al. (2024) showed that examples containing new facts are learned much more slowly than familiar ones, and that once learned they raise the model's tendency to hallucinate. The rule of thumb: \u003cb>fine-tune for behaviour, retrieve for facts\u003c/b>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The ladder, cheapest rung first\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Step\u003c/th>\u003cth>Typical effort\u003c/th>\u003cth>What it fixes\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Clearer instructions and a few examples\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hours\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Most format, tone and scope problems\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Retrieval or tools\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Days\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Missing, fresh or private knowledge\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>A stronger or reasoning model\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Minutes, but higher cost per call\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hard reasoning\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Supervised fine-tuning, usually LoRA\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Days to weeks, mostly data work\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Consistent behaviour, narrow skills, shorter prompts, a smaller model\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Preference or reinforcement tuning\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Weeks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Quality judgements; skills with a checkable answer\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>When fine-tuning pays off\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Good reasons, roughly in order of how often teams hit them:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cost and latency at volume.\u003c/b> Move a narrow task from a frontier model to a small model (distillation, covered later in this module).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Consistency on a narrow task.\u003c/b> Classification with your own labels, extraction to a strict schema, a format the model keeps breaking.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Behaviour that is easier to show than describe.\u003c/b> House style, domain shorthand, how an expert triages.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Replacing a long prompt.\u003c/b> Thirty few-shot examples baked into weights mean a 300-token prompt instead of 4,000 tokens on every call.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Data or deployment constraints.\u003c/b> The model must run on-device or inside your network, so a small open model must do a big model's job.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Skills with a verifier.\u003c/b> If code can check the answer (tests pass, SQL returns the right rows), reinforcement fine-tuning can push past what demonstrations teach.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Bad reasons: teaching documentation that changes; fixing a problem you have not measured; having fewer than about a hundred good examples; wanting the model to be \"smarter in general\".\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Worked example: does the volume justify it?\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Task: classify and extract fields from support emails\nFrontier model with a long prompt: 1,500 input + 200 output tokens\n  at $3 / $15 per million tokens: (1,500 × 3 + 200 × 15) / 1,000,000 = $0.0075 per email\nFine-tuned small model with a short prompt: 300 input + 200 output tokens\n  at $0.20 / $0.80 per million:   (300 × 0.20 + 200 × 0.80) / 1,000,000 = $0.00022 per email\n\n2,000,000 emails a month: $15,000 versus $440   saves about $14,500 a month\n   20,000 emails a month:    $150 versus   $4.40 saves about $145 a month\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>At two million emails the savings pay for data labelling, training, evals and hosting within weeks. At twenty thousand they never repay the engineering time. The prices are illustrative; the shape of the argument is what matters.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Hidden costs\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Data work dominates.\u003c/b> Collecting, cleaning and labelling takes far longer than training.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Maintenance.\u003c/b> When a better base model ships, you re-train. Hosted fine-tunes die with their base model, and as of late 2026 OpenAI is winding down self-serve fine-tuning entirely (closed to new organisations since May 2026, no new jobs for anyone after 6 January 2027).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Serving.\u003c/b> A fine-tuned open model must be hosted somewhere (the Serving module).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Regressions.\u003c/b> Fine-tuning can quietly damage general ability and safety behaviour (a later lesson).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Strong teams build the eval set (50 to 200 real examples) and the \u003cb>best prompted baseline\u003c/b> with a frontier model first. If the baseline is good enough, they ship it. If it is good but too expensive or slow, its outputs become training data for a small model. Typical fine-tunes in 2026: routers and classifiers, extraction, tool calling for small agent models, on-device models, house style, and reinforcement tuning for verifiable skills such as code and SQL.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Decide whether to prompt, retrieve or fine-tune in three scenarios, and justify each: (a) an HR assistant must answer from 800 policy pages that change monthly; (b) 3 million invoices a month need 25 fields extracted into a fixed JSON schema, and a prompted frontier model already gets 97% right but costs too much; (c) marketing wants product descriptions in the brand voice, and a prompt with five examples already gets most of the way.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>(a) Retrieval. It is a knowledge problem with facts that change, so fine-tuning would freeze outdated policies and raise hallucination risk; retrieval with citations stays current by re-indexing. (b) Fine-tune, by distillation. The behaviour is well defined and measurable, volume is high, and the frontier model's corrected outputs make a ready training set; a small fine-tuned model with a short prompt can match its quality at a fraction of the cost, which you confirm on a held-out eval. (c) Prompting. Improve the style guide and examples first; consider fine-tuning only if evals show prompting has plateaued and volume makes it worthwhile.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is fine-tuning a poor way to teach a model your product catalogue?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Models learn new facts slowly and unreliably through fine-tuning, and research found that training on new facts increases hallucination. The catalogue also changes, which would mean re-training every time, while retrieval supplies current facts with citations at request time.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A prompted frontier model costs $0.006 per request and a fine-tuned small model would cost $0.0004. Building and maintaining the fine-tune costs about $4,000 a month. At what monthly volume do you break even?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Saving per request is $0.006 − $0.0004 = $0.0056, so break-even is 4,000 / 0.0056 ≈ 714,000 requests a month. Below that, keep the prompted model.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What should exist before you build any training data, and why?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>An eval set of real examples and the best prompted baseline measured on it. Without them you cannot tell whether fine-tuning helps, and the baseline's outputs are often the starting point for the training data.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:preparing-supervised-fine-tuning-data":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Training data is the specification of a fine-tune: the model learns exactly what the examples show, including their mistakes. The best source is your own \u003cb>production traces\u003c/b>, filtered to good outcomes, corrected by people, scrubbed of personal data, deduplicated and written in the model's \u003cb>chat format\u003c/b>. Split off the eval set before anything else so nothing leaks.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The format: chat messages, one example per line\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Fine-tuning data uses the same \u003ccode>messages\u003c/code> structure as API calls, stored as JSONL (one JSON object per line):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">json\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>{\"messages\": [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"Classify the ticket. Reply with JSON only.\"}, {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"I was charged twice for order 8812.\"}, {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": \"{\\\"category\\\": \\\"billing\\\", \\\"priority\\\": \\\"high\\\"}\"}]}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>TRL also accepts a \u003cb>prompt-completion\u003c/b> form, \u003ccode>{\"prompt\": [...messages...], \"completion\": [...assistant message...]}\u003c/code>, which makes it explicit which part is the answer.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Two details decide whether training works:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>The chat template.\u003c/b> Before training, the trainer turns the messages into the exact token string the model was built with, including special tokens such as \u003ccode>&lt;|im_start|&gt;assistant\u003c/code>. Use the model's own template, and serve with the same one. A template mismatch between training and serving is the most common silent fine-tuning bug: the model never learnt to stop, or answers in a format your server does not parse.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Loss masking.\u003c/b> You want the model to learn to write the assistant turns, not to predict the user's words. TRL computes loss only on the completion for prompt-completion data, and on assistant turns only when you set \u003ccode>assistant_only_loss=True\u003c/code> for conversational data.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>From traces to a training set\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Export traces\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Keep good outcomes\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Fix and scrub\u003c/b>\u003csmall>corrections, PII\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Deduplicate\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Split eval first\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Write chat JSONL\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Export with metadata:\u003c/b> input, output, prompt version, model, user or account id, timestamp, user feedback, automatic check results.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Keep good outcomes.\u003c/b> Thumbs-up, answers accepted without edits, outputs that passed checks, or downstream success (the ticket was not reopened). When a person edited the output, train on the edited version; keep the original too, since original and edit make a preference pair for DPO later.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Fix rather than only filter.\u003c/b> If you keep only the cases the model already got right, you train on easy examples. Have a domain expert correct a sample of failures and hard cases, guided by a short labelling guide so two annotators would agree.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Scrub.\u003c/b> Remove or consistently replace personal data and secrets. Use the system prompt you will actually serve.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Deduplicate.\u003c/b> Remove exact duplicates (hash of normalised text) and near-duplicates (MinHash or embedding similarity). Cap templated clusters: if \"reset my password\" is 30% of traffic, keep a few hundred, not thirty thousand.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cover the space.\u003c/b> Stratify by category and include rare cases, refusals and \"not enough information\" answers.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Split by user or by time,\u003c/b> not by random row. Paraphrases from the same customer or week otherwise land on both sides and inflate eval scores.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Check lengths.\u003c/b> Count tokens; examples longer than the training \u003ccode>max_length\u003c/code> are truncated, which can cut off the very answer you want to teach.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>A sketch of the pipeline\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import hashlib, json, random\n\ndef norm(s): return \" \".join(s.lower().split())\n\nrows = [json.loads(line) for line in open(\"traces.jsonl\")]\ngood = [r for r in rows if r[\"feedback\"] == \"up\" or r[\"accepted_without_edit\"]]\n\nseen, unique = set(), []\nfor r in good:\n    h = hashlib.sha256(norm(r[\"input\"]).encode()).hexdigest()\n    if h not in seen:\n        seen.add(h)\n        unique.append(r)\n\nusers = sorted({r[\"user_id\"] for r in unique})\nrandom.Random(0).shuffle(users)\neval_users = set(users[: len(users) // 10])          # 10% of users, not 10% of rows\n\nSYSTEM = open(\"prompts/classifier_v3.txt\").read()     # the prompt you will serve\ndef to_chat(r):\n    answer = r.get(\"edited_output\") or r[\"output\"]\n    return {\"messages\": [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": SYSTEM},\n                         {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": r[\"input\"]},\n                         {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": answer}]}\n\nwith open(\"train.jsonl\", \"w\") as tr, open(\"eval.jsonl\", \"w\") as ev:\n    for r in unique:\n        (ev if r[\"user_id\"] in eval_users else tr).write(json.dumps(to_chat(r)) + \"\\n\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The field names are whatever your trace store uses; the order of steps is the point.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How many examples?\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Unsloth's guide suggests at least about 100 rows for reasonable results and over 1,000 for good ones. The LIMA paper (2023) fine-tuned a 65B model on just 1,000 carefully curated examples and got strong instruction following, which shows how far quality goes. For narrow classification or extraction tasks, 500 to 5,000 clean examples is typical. Five hundred correct examples beat five thousand noisy ones.\u003c/p>\u003cp>To find out whether more data would help, train on 25%, 50% and 100% of your set and plot the eval score. If it is still climbing at 100%, collecting more is worth it; if it has flattened, work on quality or the method instead.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Training on raw model outputs nobody checked copies the model's mistakes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Inconsistent labels (two annotators, two conventions) teach noise; write the guide first.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Changing the system prompt between training and production shifts behaviour.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Customer data needs a lawful basis and must respect contracts; record sources, filters, counts and dates in a short \u003cb>data card\u003c/b>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you build 500 high-quality chat-format examples from production traces?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Export a few thousand traces with metadata; keep those with positive signals (thumbs-up, accepted unedited, passed checks) and have an expert correct a sample of failures and hard cases using a labelling guide; scrub personal data; remove exact and near-duplicates and cap templated clusters; split off the eval set by user or time first; write each example as \u003ccode>messages\u003c/code> JSONL with the production system prompt and the corrected assistant reply; check token lengths against \u003ccode>max_length\u003c/code>; and record a data card. Expect filtering to discard most raw traces, so start with several times more than 500.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why split train and eval by user or time instead of by random row?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Similar requests from the same user or period would otherwise appear on both sides, so the model is tested on near-copies of its training data and the score overstates real performance.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is a chat template, and what goes wrong if it differs between training and serving?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It is the model-specific way messages are rendered into tokens, with role markers and end-of-turn tokens. If training and serving differ, the model may not stop where expected, may leak role markers, or may produce output the server's parser cannot read.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">You have 3,000 examples. How do you tell whether collecting more would help?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Train on subsets (for example 750, 1,500 and 3,000) and plot the eval score. A curve still rising at 3,000 suggests more data helps; a flat curve says improve quality, coverage or the method instead.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:lora-and-qlora":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>LoRA\u003c/b> (low-rank adaptation) freezes the model and trains two thin matrices beside each weight matrix, so you update well under 1% of the parameters and save a small adapter file. \u003cb>QLoRA\u003c/b> also stores the frozen model in 4-bit, which cuts memory enough to fine-tune an 8B model on one consumer GPU. For typical post-training dataset sizes, a well-configured LoRA matches full fine-tuning.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>How LoRA works\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A weight matrix \u003ccode>W\u003c/code> of shape d × k would get a full-size update during full fine-tuning. LoRA instead learns the update as the product of two small matrices, with a rank \u003ccode>r\u003c/code> that is typically 8 to 64:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>W' = W + (alpha / r) × B × A\nA: r × k, random at start      B: d × r, zeros at start, so W' = W at step 0\nOnly A and B are trained; W never changes.\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>For the attention query projection of a Llama-3.1-8B-shaped model, \u003ccode>W\u003c/code> is 4,096 × 4,096 = 16,777,216 numbers. A rank-16 LoRA adds 16 × (4,096 + 4,096) = 131,072 trainable numbers, 0.78% of that matrix. It works because the update fine-tuning needs has low \"intrinsic rank\": a few directions carry most of the change. The original LoRA paper reported that, compared with fine-tuning GPT-3 175B with Adam, LoRA cut trainable parameters by 10,000 times and GPU memory by 3 times, with no extra inference latency once merged.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked example: an 8B model\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Llama 3.1 8B has 32 layers, hidden size 4,096, MLP size 14,336, and grouped-query attention whose key and value projections output 1,024 values. LoRA at r = 16 on all seven linear layers in each block:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Module\u003c/th>\u003cth>Shape (in × out)\u003c/th>\u003cth>LoRA parameters, r = 16\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>q_proj, o_proj\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4,096 × 4,096\u003c/td>\u003ctd>131,072 each\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>k_proj, v_proj\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4,096 × 1,024\u003c/td>\u003ctd>81,920 each\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>gate_proj, up_proj, down_proj\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4,096 × 14,336\u003c/td>\u003ctd>294,912 each\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>One layer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1,310,720\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>32 layers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>41,943,040 (0.52% of 8.03B)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Rank scales the count linearly: r = 8 gives about 21M, r = 64 about 168M. Adapting only q and v, as the original paper did, gives 6.8M at r = 16. The saved adapter at r = 16 is about 84 MB in 16-bit, against 16 GB for the full model.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Settings that matter\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Target modules:\u003c/b> all linear layers (\u003ccode>target_modules=\"all-linear\"\u003c/code> in PEFT). Thinking Machines' 2025 study \u003ci>LoRA without regret\u003c/i> found attention-only LoRA underperforms even when given the same parameter count, and that LoRA on all layers, especially the MLPs, matched full fine-tuning for small and medium datasets.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Rank:\u003c/b> 16 or 32 is the usual start. Reinforcement learning needs very little capacity (the same study found even rank 1 matched full fine-tuning for RL); very large SFT datasets may need more.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Alpha:\u003c/b> scales the update by alpha / r. Common choices are alpha = r or 2r.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Learning rate:\u003c/b> about 10 times the full fine-tuning rate. Start at 2e-4 (Unsloth's recommendation) or 1e-4 (TRL's suggestion) for LoRA SFT.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Batch size:\u003c/b> LoRA tolerates very large batches less well than full fine-tuning; effective batches of 8 to 32 are typical.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>QLoRA: a 4-bit frozen base\u003c/h4>\u003cp>QLoRA keeps the frozen weights in a 4-bit format and dequantises each layer to 16-bit on the fly for the forward and backward pass; gradients flow through the frozen weights into 16-bit LoRA matrices. Its three tricks: \u003cb>NF4\u003c/b>, a 4-bit data type suited to normally distributed weights; \u003cb>double quantisation\u003c/b>, which quantises the per-block scaling constants too (from 0.5 to about 0.127 extra bits per parameter, roughly 3 GB saved on a 65B model); and \u003cb>paged optimisers\u003c/b> that absorb memory spikes. The paper fine-tuned a 65B model on one 48 GB GPU and its Guanaco models reached 99.3% of ChatGPT's level on the Vicuna benchmark after 24 hours on one GPU.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Memory for a Llama-3.1-8B-shaped model, sequence 2,048, batch 1, gradient checkpointing\n\nFull fine-tune, AdamW in mixed precision (16 bytes per parameter):\n  8.03B × 16 bytes                                    = 128 GB before activations\n\nLoRA on a 16-bit base:\n  frozen weights 8.03B × 2 bytes                      =  16.1 GB\n  adapter 42M × 16 bytes (fp32 weight, grad, Adam)    =   0.7 GB\n  activations                                         ≈   2 GB     total ≈ 19-20 GB\n\nQLoRA:\n  6.98B linear-layer weights × 0.5 bytes (NF4)        =   3.5 GB\n  quantisation constants (double quantised)           =   0.1 GB\n  embeddings and LM head kept in 16-bit, 1.05B × 2    =   2.1 GB\n  adapter, gradients, optimiser                       =   0.7 GB\n  activations incl. 2,048 × 128,256 fp32 logits       ≈   2 GB\n  CUDA context and fragmentation                      ≈   1 GB     total ≈ 9-10 GB\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Unsloth's requirements table lists about 6 GB (QLoRA) and 22 GB (16-bit LoRA) as absolute minimums for an 8B model, because its kernels avoid materialising the full logits and offload activations. Either way, a QLoRA run on an 8B model fits a 12 to 16 GB GPU, and comfortably fits 24 GB.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import torch\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, BitsAndBytesConfig\nfrom peft import LoraConfig, get_peft_model, prepare_model_for_kbit_training\n\nbnb = BitsAndBytesConfig(load_in_4bit=True, bnb_4bit_quant_type=\"nf4\",\n                         bnb_4bit_use_double_quant=True, bnb_4bit_compute_dtype=torch.bfloat16)\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\"&lt;8b-model&gt;\", quantization_config=bnb, device_map=\"auto\")\nmodel = prepare_model_for_kbit_training(model)        # enables gradient checkpointing, casts norms\nlora = LoraConfig(r=16, lora_alpha=32, lora_dropout=0.0, target_modules=\"all-linear\", task_type=\"CAUSAL_LM\")\nmodel = get_peft_model(model, lora)\nmodel.print_trainable_parameters()                     # about 42M trainable for a Llama-3.1-8B shape\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>After training: merge or serve adapters\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Merge\u003c/b> computes \u003ccode>W + (alpha / r) × B × A\u003c/code> once, giving an ordinary model with no extra latency. Merge into the 16-bit base (not the 4-bit one), then quantise the result for serving if needed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Multi-LoRA serving\u003c/b> keeps one base model in memory and loads many adapters: vLLM's \u003ccode>--enable-lora --lora-modules name=path\u003c/code> and SGLang can serve per-customer or per-task adapters from one GPU.\u003c/li>\u003cli>An adapter only works with the exact base model it was trained on. Record the base model id and revision.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Variants exist (DoRA, rsLoRA; \u003ccode>use_dora\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>use_rslora\u003c/code> in PEFT) but plain LoRA on all linear layers is the sensible default.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What are the steps and settings to fine-tune a 1B to 8B model with QLoRA on one GPU?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Load the model in 4-bit NF4 with double quantisation and bf16 compute (bitsandbytes, or Unsloth's \u003ccode>load_in_4bit=True\u003c/code>); attach LoRA to all linear layers with r = 16 and alpha = 16 or 32; train with SFT at a learning rate around 1e-4 to 2e-4 for 1 to 3 epochs, batch 1 to 4 per device with gradient accumulation to an effective 8 to 32, gradient checkpointing on and \u003ccode>max_length\u003c/code> set from your data's token lengths; evaluate on the held-out set; save the adapter, then merge it into the 16-bit base or serve it as a LoRA adapter. An 8B model needs roughly 6 to 10 GB, so a 16 GB or 24 GB GPU works; a 1B to 3B model fits a free 16 GB notebook GPU.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How many trainable parameters does a rank-8 LoRA add to one 4,096 × 14,336 MLP projection?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>8 × (4,096 + 14,336) = 8 × 18,432 = 147,456 parameters.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does QLoRA need far less memory than 16-bit LoRA, and what does it cost?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The frozen weights, which dominate memory, are stored at about 4 bits instead of 16, roughly a quarter of the size. The cost is extra compute to dequantise each layer on the fly (somewhat slower steps) and a small potential quality loss from quantisation.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is full fine-tuning of an 8B model so much more expensive than LoRA?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Full fine-tuning keeps gradients and Adam optimiser state for every parameter, about 16 bytes per parameter or 128 GB for 8B, which needs several large GPUs; LoRA keeps those only for about 42M adapter parameters.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:supervised-fine-tuning-with-trl":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Supervised fine-tuning (SFT)\u003c/b> teaches a model to imitate your examples by minimising next-token cross-entropy on the answer tokens. With Hugging Face's \u003cb>TRL\u003c/b> library it is one trainer, one config and a LoRA config. The script is short; the craft is in choosing settings from your data, reading the loss curves, and measuring the same held-out eval before and after.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What SFT optimises\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each training example, the model predicts every answer token given all the tokens before it, and the loss is the average negative log-probability of the correct token. A loss of 0.5 means the model gave the right next token an average probability of about e^-0.5 ≈ 0.61. Prompt tokens are masked out, so the model learns to write answers, not to predict users. TRL also logs \u003ccode>mean_token_accuracy\u003c/code>, the share of answer tokens the model would have predicted exactly.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>A complete training script\u003c/h4>\u003cp>This targets TRL 1.x with Transformers 5 (late 2026). It continues from the JSONL files built in the data lesson.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import torch\nfrom datasets import load_dataset\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer, BitsAndBytesConfig\nfrom peft import LoraConfig\nfrom trl import SFTConfig, SFTTrainer\n\nMODEL = \"&lt;small-instruct-model&gt;\"                     # a 1B to 8B open model\ndata = load_dataset(\"json\", data_files={\"train\": \"train.jsonl\", \"eval\": \"eval.jsonl\"})\n\ntokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(MODEL)\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\n    MODEL, dtype=torch.bfloat16, device_map=\"auto\",\n    quantization_config=BitsAndBytesConfig(load_in_4bit=True, bnb_4bit_quant_type=\"nf4\",\n                                           bnb_4bit_compute_dtype=torch.bfloat16))\n\nargs = SFTConfig(\n    output_dir=\"out/ticket-classifier\",\n    num_train_epochs=2,\n    per_device_train_batch_size=4,\n    gradient_accumulation_steps=4,       # effective batch of 16\n    learning_rate=1e-4,\n    lr_scheduler_type=\"cosine\",\n    warmup_steps=0.05,                   # a float below 1 means a fraction of total steps\n    max_length=2048,                     # set from your token-length statistics\n    assistant_only_loss=True,            # loss on assistant turns only\n    eval_strategy=\"steps\", eval_steps=50,\n    save_strategy=\"steps\", save_steps=50,\n    logging_steps=10,\n)\ntrainer = SFTTrainer(\n    model=model, args=args, processing_class=tokenizer,\n    train_dataset=data[\"train\"], eval_dataset=data[\"eval\"],\n    peft_config=LoraConfig(r=16, lora_alpha=32, target_modules=\"all-linear\", task_type=\"CAUSAL_LM\"),\n)\ntrainer.train()\ntrainer.save_model(\"out/ticket-classifier/final\")     # writes the LoRA adapter\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Details worth knowing about current TRL:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>SFTConfig\u003c/code> defaults differ from plain Transformers: learning rate 2e-5 (meant for full fine-tuning, so raise it for LoRA), gradient checkpointing on and bf16 on. Its \u003ccode>max_length\u003c/code> defaults to 1024; set it deliberately, since too short silently truncates answers.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If you pass the model as a string, TRL loads it in float32 unless you set \u003ccode>model_init_kwargs={\"dtype\": torch.bfloat16}\u003c/code>; loading it yourself, as above, avoids the surprise.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>assistant_only_loss=True\u003c/code> needs a chat template with generation markers. TRL patches known families such as Qwen3; for others, use prompt-completion data, where completion-only loss is the default.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Older tutorials use \u003ccode>max_seq_length=\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>tokenizer=\u003c/code>; these are now \u003ccode>max_length\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>processing_class\u003c/code>. Free notebook GPUs such as the T4 lack bf16, so there you set \u003ccode>fp16=True\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>bf16=False\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Reading the curves\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>What you see\u003c/th>\u003cth>What it usually means\u003c/th>\u003cth>What to do\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Train and eval loss fall, then flatten together\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Healthy\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Stop at the flat point\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Train loss keeps falling, eval loss turns upward\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Overfitting\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fewer epochs, more or cleaner data, keep an earlier checkpoint\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Train loss near zero after one epoch on a small set\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Memorisation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Lower learning rate or epochs; check for duplicates\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Loss spikes or becomes NaN\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Learning rate too high, bad example, fp16 overflow\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Lower the rate; inspect the batch; prefer bf16\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Loss barely moves\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Rate too low, or masking or template bug\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Decode one training batch and read it\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Loss is a proxy. The decision comes from the task eval.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Evaluating before and after\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Score the base model and the fine-tune on the same held-out examples with the same metric. With PEFT, you can switch the adapter off to get the base model without loading a second copy:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import json\n\ndef generate(m, messages):\n    text = tokenizer.apply_chat_template(messages, tokenize=False, add_generation_prompt=True)\n    inputs = tokenizer(text, return_tensors=\"pt\").to(m.device)\n    out = m.generate(**inputs, max_new_tokens=128, do_sample=False)\n    return tokenizer.decode(out[0][inputs[\"input_ids\"].shape[1]:], skip_special_tokens=True)\n\ndef exact_json(m):\n    hits = 0\n    for ex in data[\"eval\"]:\n        prompt, gold = ex[\"messages\"][:-1], ex[\"messages\"][-1][\"content\"]\n        try:\n            hits += json.loads(generate(m, prompt)) == json.loads(gold)\n        except json.JSONDecodeError:\n            pass\n    return hits / len(data[\"eval\"])\n\npeft_model = trainer.model.eval()\nwith peft_model.disable_adapter():\n    print(\"base\", exact_json(peft_model))\nprint(\"fine-tuned\", exact_json(peft_model))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>For hundreds or thousands of eval items, serve the adapter with vLLM (\u003ccode>--enable-lora\u003c/code>) and call it through the OpenAI-compatible API, which is much faster than \u003ccode>generate\u003c/code> in a loop. Always add a third row to the comparison: the best prompted frontier model.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Common bugs\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>The model never stops.\u003c/b> The end-of-turn token is missing from the training targets, or the serving template differs.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>It echoes the user or the system prompt.\u003c/b> Loss was computed on prompt tokens.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Great eval loss, poor answers.\u003c/b> Eval items leaked into training, or loss and task metric disagree; read samples.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Worse than the base model.\u003c/b> Learning rate too high, too many epochs, or inconsistent labels.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you train with SFTTrainer and evaluate the model before and after?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Load chat-format train and eval JSONL with \u003ccode>datasets\u003c/code>; load the model (4-bit for QLoRA) and its tokeniser; create an \u003ccode>SFTConfig\u003c/code> with a LoRA-appropriate learning rate (about 1e-4), 1 to 3 epochs, an effective batch of about 16, \u003ccode>max_length\u003c/code> from your data and assistant-only or completion-only loss; pass the model, config, datasets and a \u003ccode>LoraConfig\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>SFTTrainer\u003c/code> and call \u003ccode>train()\u003c/code>. Then run the same held-out eval with the same metric on the base model (adapter disabled), the fine-tune and the best prompted frontier model, and compare quality, latency and cost.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your eval loss starts rising after the first epoch while training loss keeps falling. What happened and what do you do?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The model is overfitting the training set. Keep the checkpoint from the end of the first epoch, reduce epochs or learning rate, and add more varied or cleaner data.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why should the comparison include a prompted frontier model and not just the base small model?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Beating the small base model only shows the fine-tune learnt something. The business question is whether it matches the best alternative you could ship, usually a well-prompted frontier model, at lower cost or latency.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:preference-tuning-with-dpo":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>DPO\u003c/b> (direct preference optimisation) teaches from pairs: for the same prompt, a \u003cb>chosen\u003c/b> and a \u003cb>rejected\u003c/b> answer. It makes the chosen answer more likely relative to the rejected one, measured against a frozen \u003cb>reference\u003c/b> copy of the model, with no separate reward model and no RL loop. Use it after SFT for things that are easier to judge than to write: tone, verbosity, hedging, over-refusal, a recurring subtle error.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>When DPO beats more SFT\u003c/h4>\u003cp>SFT shows one good answer. DPO shows \"this, not that\", which carries information SFT cannot: what to avoid. It suits problems where people find it easy to say which of two answers is better but hard to write the perfect one. It needs a model that is already roughly right, so it usually runs on top of an SFT model or a capable instruct model. (The theory, RLHF and DPO's derivation are in the training module.)\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Building pairs\u003c/h4>\u003cp>TRL's recommended conversational format:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">json\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>{\"prompt\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Summarise this incident for the on-call engineer: ...\"}],\n \"chosen\": [{\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": \"EU writes failing since 02:14 UTC after DB failover. Rollback in progress. Next update 02:45.\"}],\n \"rejected\": [{\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": \"Thanks for reaching out! It sounds like you may be experiencing some issues with the database...\"}]}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Where pairs come from, best first:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Human edits.\u003c/b> The model's original answer is rejected, the person's edited version is chosen. Cheap and on-target.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Sample and rank.\u003c/b> Generate 2 to 4 answers per prompt from your current model at temperature 0.8 to 1.0, then rank them with people or with an LLM judge validated against human labels (the Evals module). Take best versus worst.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Thumbs up and down.\u003c/b> Unpaired good and bad labels; KTO is designed for that shape of data.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Constructed negatives.\u003c/b> Deliberately flawed answers. Use sparingly: pairs that are too easy teach little.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>\u003cb>On-policy\u003c/b> pairs, sampled from the model you are training, work better than pairs written by another model, because DPO adjusts the probabilities of answers your model actually produces. Make pairs differ in the property you care about and nothing else. If chosen answers are systematically longer, the model learns \"longer is better\"; compare the length distributions of both sides before training.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How the loss works, with numbers\u003c/h4>\u003cp>DPO gives each answer an \u003cb>implicit reward\u003c/b>: β times how much more likely the trained model makes it than the reference model does (a difference of log-probabilities). The loss is \u003ccode>-log(sigmoid(reward_chosen - reward_rejected))\u003c/code>. With β = 0.1, TRL's default:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Change in log-prob vs reference: chosen, rejected\u003c/th>\u003cth>Rewards: chosen, rejected\u003c/th>\u003cth>Margin\u003c/th>\u003cth>Loss\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>0, 0 (start of training)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.00, 0.00\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.00\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.693\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>+2.0, −1.0\u003c/td>\u003ctd>+0.20, −0.10\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.30\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.554\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>+5.0, −15.0\u003c/td>\u003ctd>+0.50, −1.50\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2.00\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.127\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>−3.0, −13.0\u003c/td>\u003ctd>−0.30, −1.30\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1.00\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.313\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The last row is the trap. The loss fell although the chosen answer became \u003ci>less\u003c/i> likely, because the rejected one fell faster. TRL's own docs note that DPO usually works by pushing rejected answers down rather than chosen ones up. If both sink a long way, output quality drifts. \u003cb>β\u003c/b> sets how far the model may move from the reference: higher β keeps it closer.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Running DPO with TRL\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from datasets import load_dataset\nfrom peft import LoraConfig\nfrom trl import DPOConfig, DPOTrainer\n\npairs = load_dataset(\"json\", data_files=\"pairs.jsonl\", split=\"train\")\nargs = DPOConfig(output_dir=\"out/dpo\", beta=0.1, learning_rate=1e-5, num_train_epochs=1,\n                 per_device_train_batch_size=2, gradient_accumulation_steps=8, max_length=2048)\ntrainer = DPOTrainer(model=sft_model, args=args, train_dataset=pairs, processing_class=tokenizer,\n                     peft_config=LoraConfig(r=16, lora_alpha=32, target_modules=\"all-linear\",\n                                            task_type=\"CAUSAL_LM\"))\ntrainer.train()\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>With a LoRA config and no \u003ccode>ref_model\u003c/code>, the reference is the same network with the adapter switched off, so you do not hold two copies in memory. TRL's default learning rate for DPO is 1e-6, which suits full fine-tuning; LoRA typically wants about ten times more. One epoch is often enough.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What to watch\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>rewards/accuracies\u003c/code>: the share of pairs where chosen beats rejected. It should climb well above 0.5.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>rewards/margins\u003c/code>: should grow steadily, not explode.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>logps/chosen\u003c/code>: if it falls steadily, the model is drifting; lower the learning rate, raise β, or add an SFT term (\u003ccode>loss_type=[\"sigmoid\", \"sft\"]\u003c/code> in TRL).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Your task eval and a sample of real outputs, side by side with the SFT model. Metrics on the training pairs can look perfect while answers get worse.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Variants you will meet: \u003cb>IPO\u003c/b> (more robust to noisy labels), \u003cb>KTO\u003c/b> (unpaired thumbs), \u003cb>ORPO\u003c/b> and \u003cb>SimPO\u003c/b> (no reference model). Plain DPO with good on-policy pairs is the default to beat.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you build chosen and rejected pairs and run DPO?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Start from an SFT or instruct model. Collect prompts from real traffic; get pairs from human edits (original rejected, edit chosen) or by sampling 2 to 4 answers per prompt from the current model and ranking them with people or a validated judge, keeping best versus worst; make the pair differ in the target property and check that lengths are balanced. Write them as prompt, chosen and rejected in conversational format, then train with \u003ccode>DPOTrainer\u003c/code> and a LoRA config, β about 0.1, a learning rate around 1e-5 for LoRA and about one epoch. Watch reward accuracy and margins, check that \u003ccode>logps/chosen\u003c/code> is not collapsing, and compare outputs and the task eval against the SFT model.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Training loss and reward accuracy look great, but answers got worse. What is a likely cause?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The model lowered the probability of both chosen and rejected answers, with rejected falling faster, so the margin grew while the model drifted away from good outputs. Another cause is a spurious pattern in the pairs, such as chosen answers always being longer.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why are on-policy pairs better than pairs written by another model?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>DPO shifts probability between answers the model itself is likely to produce. Pairs from a different model may sit in regions your model rarely visits, so pushing on them changes little about its real behaviour.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:reinforcement-fine-tuning-with-grpo-and-verifier":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Reinforcement fine-tuning\u003c/b> lets a model learn from a score instead of from written examples. For each prompt it tries several answers, a \u003cb>verifier\u003c/b> (code that checks the answer) scores them, and the model is pushed towards answers that scored above the group's average. \u003cb>GRPO\u003c/b> (group relative policy optimisation), introduced with DeepSeekMath and used for DeepSeek-R1, does this without a separate value model. It shines when you can check answers automatically but cannot easily write perfect demonstrations.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>When RL beats SFT\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use reinforcement fine-tuning when:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>You have a reliable checker:\u003c/b> unit tests pass, SQL returns the expected rows, JSON matches the gold fields, a maths answer equals the reference, a tool call succeeds.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The path to the answer is not known in advance.\u003c/b> The model can discover strategies (longer reasoning, checking its work) that nobody wrote down.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Demonstrations are expensive but checking is cheap.\u003c/b>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Avoid it when there is no trustworthy reward (subjective quality is better served by DPO or a carefully validated judge), or when the model almost never succeeds, since there is then nothing to reinforce.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The GRPO loop\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Prompt\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Sample a group\u003c/b>\u003csmall>e.g. 8 answers\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Score each\u003c/b>\u003csmall>reward functions\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Advantage\u003c/b>\u003csmall>reward minus group mean\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Update\u003c/b>\u003csmall>raise above-average, lower below-average\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A worked group. The model writes 8 SQL queries for one question; 2 pass the check:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>rewards     = [1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0]       group mean = 0.25\nadvantages  = reward - mean = [+0.75, -0.25, -0.25, -0.25, +0.75, -0.25, -0.25, -0.25]\nscaled by the group's standard deviation (0.433): about +1.73 for passes, -0.58 for failures\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Every token of a passing answer is nudged up, every token of a failing one down. Dividing by the standard deviation is TRL's default (\u003ccode>scale_rewards=\"group\"\u003c/code>); the Dr. GRPO paper argues for not dividing, and TRL supports both. Because the baseline is simply the group mean, GRPO needs no learned critic network, which is the memory saving over PPO.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>The difficulty sweet spot.\u003c/b> If all 8 answers pass, or all fail, every advantage is zero and the prompt teaches nothing. Before training, sample the starting model on each prompt and drop those it always or never solves. TRL logs \u003ccode>frac_reward_zero_std\u003c/code>, the share of groups with no signal; if it is high, your data is too easy or too hard.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Writing reward functions\u003c/h4>\u003cp>In TRL a reward function receives the completions plus any dataset columns as keyword arguments and returns one float per completion. For conversational data each completion is a list of messages.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import re, sqlite3\n\ndef run(db_path, sql):\n    try:\n        con = sqlite3.connect(f\"file:{db_path}?mode=ro\", uri=True)    # read-only copy\n        return sorted(con.execute(sql).fetchall())\n    except sqlite3.Error:\n        return None\n\ndef correct_rows(completions, expected_rows, db_path, **kwargs):\n    rewards = []\n    for c, expected, db in zip(completions, expected_rows, db_path):\n        blocks = re.findall(r\"```sql\\n(.*?)```\", c[0][\"content\"], re.S)\n        rows = run(db, blocks[0]) if len(blocks) == 1 else None          # exactly one block\n        rewards.append(1.0 if rows is not None and rows == sorted(map(tuple, expected)) else 0.0)\n    return rewards\n\ndef one_sql_block(completions, **kwargs):\n    return [0.2 if len(re.findall(r\"```sql\\n.*?```\", c[0][\"content\"], re.S)) == 1 else 0.0\n            for c in completions]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from peft import LoraConfig\nfrom trl import GRPOConfig, GRPOTrainer\n\nargs = GRPOConfig(output_dir=\"out/grpo-sql\", num_generations=8, max_completion_length=512,\n                  learning_rate=1e-5, per_device_train_batch_size=8, gradient_accumulation_steps=4,\n                  use_vllm=True, vllm_mode=\"colocate\", logging_steps=5)\ntrainer = GRPOTrainer(model=sft_model, reward_funcs=[correct_rows, one_sql_block], args=args,\n                      train_dataset=prompts,          # columns: prompt, expected_rows, db_path\n                      peft_config=LoraConfig(r=16, lora_alpha=32, target_modules=\"all-linear\",\n                                             task_type=\"CAUSAL_LM\"))\ntrainer.train()\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Defaults worth knowing in TRL 1.x: 8 generations per prompt, 512-token completions, no KL penalty (\u003ccode>beta=0.0\u003c/code>, so no reference model is loaded; DeepSeek-R1 used 0.001), and a \u003ccode>loss_type=\"dapo\"\u003c/code> normalisation that removes a length bias in the original GRPO loss. The effective batch must divide evenly by \u003ccode>num_generations\u003c/code>. Generation dominates the run time, which is why \u003ccode>use_vllm=True\u003c/code> matters. LoRA works well here: RL needs very little adapter capacity.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Reward hacking\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The model optimises exactly what you score, not what you meant. Real patterns: emitting two answer blocks so a loose parser finds the right one; queries that return every row when the check only tests containment; empty but well-formatted answers when format earns reward; ever-longer answers when length correlates with reward. Defences:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>Strict parsers (exactly one block), hidden test cases, read-only sandboxes for generated code.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Small weights for format rewards compared with correctness.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Read a sample of completions every few dozen steps.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A held-out eval with stricter checks than the training reward.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Watch \u003ccode>reward\u003c/code> and each \u003ccode>rewards/&lt;name&gt;/mean\u003c/code> rising, \u003ccode>completions/mean_length\u003c/code> (sudden growth or collapse is a warning), \u003ccode>completions/clipped_ratio\u003c/code> (answers hitting the length limit) and \u003ccode>frac_reward_zero_std\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Teams use RL fine-tuning for text-to-SQL, code with tests, structured extraction with exact-match scoring, tool-using agents in simulated environments, and maths. Hosted options exist (Together AI and Fireworks run GRPO or RFT jobs, Thinking Machines' Tinker exposes the training loop as an API), but most teams start with TRL or Unsloth on one GPU and a small model, after an SFT warm-up so the model already produces the right format.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you train a model with a programmatic reward on a verifiable task?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Choose a task with an automatic checker (for example SQL checked by executing it and comparing rows); build a prompt dataset with the columns the checker needs; sample the starting model to drop prompts it always or never solves; write reward functions that return one float per completion, a strict correctness reward plus a small format reward; train with \u003ccode>GRPOTrainer\u003c/code> (about 8 generations per prompt, LoRA, vLLM for generation), usually after an SFT warm-up; monitor rewards, completion length and the share of zero-variance groups; read samples for reward hacking; and confirm the gain on a held-out eval with stricter checks.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A group of 4 answers gets rewards 0.2, 0.9, 0.5 and 0.4. What are the advantages before scaling?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The mean is 0.5, so the advantages are −0.3, +0.4, 0.0 and −0.1. The second answer is reinforced, the first and fourth are discouraged, and the third is unchanged.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does a prompt the model always solves contribute nothing to GRPO training?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>All answers get the same reward, so each equals the group mean and every advantage is zero. There is no direction to push the model.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your reward climbs quickly but the held-out eval does not improve. What do you suspect?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Reward hacking: the model found a way to score that does not reflect real correctness, such as exploiting a loose parser or a weak check. Read samples, tighten the verifier and evaluate with hidden or stricter tests.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:distillation-from-a-larger-model":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Distillation\u003c/b> transfers a large \u003cb>teacher\u003c/b> model's skill on your task to a small \u003cb>student\u003c/b>. With API models you do it by having the teacher answer real prompts, filtering the answers, and fine-tuning the student on them. Done well, a 1B to 8B student matches the teacher on one narrow task at a fraction of the cost and latency, and you keep the teacher as a fallback for the hard cases.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Three flavours\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Flavour\u003c/th>\u003cth>Student learns from\u003c/th>\u003cth>Needs\u003c/th>\u003cth>Use when\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Logit distillation (classic)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The teacher's full probability distribution over next tokens (\"soft targets\")\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Teacher logits and a shared tokeniser\u003c/td>\u003ctd>You run both models yourself\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sequence-level distillation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The teacher's generated text, via SFT\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Only API access to the teacher\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Most product work\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>On-policy distillation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The student writes answers; the teacher scores each token\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Teacher log-probabilities on student text\u003c/td>\u003ctd>You want the robustness of RL with a denser signal\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Hinton, Vinyals and Dean introduced soft-target distillation in 2015: the teacher's small probabilities on wrong answers carry information that a hard label does not (a handwritten 2 that the teacher rates as slightly similar to a 3 tells the student something about shapes). With closed APIs you rarely get full logits, so sequence-level distillation is the industry workhorse. On-policy distillation, described by Thinking Machines in 2025, samples from the student and has the teacher grade every token, which corrects the student's own mistakes rather than only showing it the teacher's paths.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Evidence that it works\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Distilling step-by-step\u003c/b> (Google, 2023) trained small T5 models on LLM-generated labels plus rationales. A 770M model beat few-shot prompted 540B PaLM on a benchmark using only 80% of the available training data, where ordinary fine-tuning of the same model could not match PaLM even with all of it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>DeepSeek-R1\u003c/b> was accompanied by six distilled dense models (1.5B to 70B, built on Qwen2.5 and Llama 3 bases), fine-tuned on reasoning samples generated by R1. Their model card reports the 32B distill outperforming OpenAI's o1-mini on various benchmarks.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>The pipeline\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Real prompts\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Teacher\u003c/b>\u003csmall>best prompt, k samples\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Filter\u003c/b>\u003csmall>verifier or judge\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Fine-tune student\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Eval vs teacher\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Ship\u003c/b>\u003csmall>student first, teacher fallback\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Collect prompts\u003c/b> from real traffic, a few thousand covering the real distribution. Hold out an eval set first.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Generate with the teacher's best setup:\u003c/b> the long prompt with every instruction and example, reasoning enabled if it helps, and 2 to 4 samples per prompt.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Filter.\u003c/b> Keep only outputs that pass checks: schema validation, tests, an LLM judge with a rubric, or agreement between samples. Keeping the best of k is called \u003cb>rejection sampling\u003c/b>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Train the student with a short prompt.\u003c/b> The student learns to behave as if it had the teacher's long prompt, which removes thousands of tokens from every future call (prompt distillation).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Evaluate student against teacher\u003c/b> on the held-out set. Look at where it falls short and add more of those cases.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Ship with a cascade.\u003c/b> The student answers first; requests that fail validation or score low confidence go to the teacher.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Teacher cost: 5,000 prompts × 3 samples × (1,500 input + 600 output tokens)\n              at $3 / $15 per million tokens\n            = 15,000 × (1,500 × 3 + 600 × 15) / 1,000,000 = 15,000 × $0.0135 = $202.50\nStudent data: about 5,000 kept examples × (300 prompt + 600 answer tokens) × 3 epochs\n            ≈ 13.5 million training tokens: a small QLoRA job on one GPU\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The teacher bill is usually trivial next to the engineering time and the savings. Prices are illustrative.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls and rules\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>The student inherits the teacher's mistakes.\u003c/b> Filtering is not optional.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Unrepresentative prompts\u003c/b> give a student that shines on the eval and fails on real traffic.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Capacity limits.\u003c/b> A 1B model cannot absorb a frontier model's general ability. Keep scope narrow; distil one task, not \"the assistant\".\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Reasoning traces.\u003c/b> Long chains of thought help small models on reasoning tasks but make every answer slower; for simple extraction, train on the final answer only.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Terms of use.\u003c/b> Check the teacher's terms: some commercial API terms restrict using outputs to build competing models, and some open-weight licences add naming or attribution rules for derivatives. Record the teacher model and version in the data card.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>TRL also includes experimental trainers for logit-level and on-policy distillation (GKD and others) when you control both models.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you generate teacher outputs and fine-tune a student?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Collect a few thousand real prompts and hold out an eval set; run the teacher with its best prompt and settings, 2 to 4 samples per prompt; filter outputs with validators, tests or a rubric judge and keep the best; write chat-format examples that pair a short student prompt with the teacher's answer; fine-tune the student with LoRA SFT; compare student and teacher on the held-out set; add data for the failure cases; and deploy the student with the teacher as a fallback for low-confidence or invalid answers.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is sequence-level distillation more common than logit distillation in product teams?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Closed API teachers rarely expose full next-token distributions and often use a different tokeniser from the student, so the practical option is to train the student on the teacher's generated text.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is prompt distillation, and what does it save?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Training the student on short-prompt inputs paired with answers the teacher produced from a long, detailed prompt. The student internalises the instructions and examples, so every production call sends far fewer input tokens.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:synthetic-data-generation-and-filtering":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Synthetic data\u003c/b> is training data written by a model. It fills gaps real traffic does not cover (rare cases, new features, data you are not allowed to train on), but raw generations are repetitive, sometimes wrong, and can leak your eval items. The value is in the filtering: validate, verify, judge, deduplicate and decontaminate, then mix with real data.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cold start:\u003c/b> a new feature with no traffic yet.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Coverage:\u003c/b> rare intents, edge cases, refusals, other languages, multi-step requests.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Privacy:\u003c/b> realistic but fake records instead of real customer data.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Distillation\u003c/b> (previous lesson) is a special case: synthetic answers to real prompts.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Preference and tool-calling data:\u003c/b> candidate answers to rank, simulated tool conversations.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Generation methods\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Method\u003c/th>\u003cth>How it works\u003c/th>\u003cth>Good for\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Seed expansion (Self-Instruct)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Show a few real examples, ask for new ones, filter\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Broadening task coverage\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Attribute grid\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Generate one example per combination of attributes (user type, issue, tone, difficulty, language)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Controlled diversity and balance\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Grounded generation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Generate questions and answers from your own documents\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Domain Q&amp;A, retrieval evals\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Magpie\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Give an aligned open model only the template text up to the user turn; it writes a plausible user request, which you then answer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Large general instruction sets\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Rejection sampling\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Generate many answers, keep those a verifier accepts\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Maths, code, SQL, extraction\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Self-Instruct (2022) showed how far this goes: fine-tuning vanilla GPT-3 on its own filtered generations gave a 33% absolute improvement on Super-NaturalInstructions, on par with InstructGPT-001.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Diversity is the main problem.\u003c/b> Ask a model for 1,000 support emails and you get the same thirty with different names. An attribute grid fixes this by construction:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import itertools, json, random\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()          # any OpenAI-compatible endpoint\ngrid = {\n    \"customer\": [\"new user\", \"enterprise admin\", \"angry long-time customer\"],\n    \"issue\": [\"double charge\", \"refund after 30 days\", \"invoice in wrong currency\", \"card declined\"],\n    \"channel\": [\"email\", \"chat\"],\n    \"clarity\": [\"clear\", \"ambiguous\", \"missing the order number\"],\n}\ncombos = list(itertools.product(*grid.values()))      # 3 × 4 × 2 × 3 = 72 combinations\nrandom.Random(0).shuffle(combos)\n\ndef generate(combo):\n    spec = dict(zip(grid, combo))\n    prompt = (f\"Write one realistic {spec['channel']} message from a {spec['customer']} about a \"\n              f\"{spec['issue']}. The request should be {spec['clarity']}. \"\n              'Return JSON: {\"message\": ..., \"category\": ...}')\n    r = client.chat.completions.create(model=\"&lt;generator-model&gt;\", temperature=1.0,\n                                       response_format={\"type\": \"json_object\"},\n                                       messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}])\n    return {**spec, **json.loads(r.choices[0].message.content)}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Other diversity levers: rotate the seed examples on every call, ask for a list of distinct scenarios first and expand each one separately, and vary length and style instructions.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The filtering pipeline\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Generate\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Validate\u003c/b>\u003csmall>schema, length\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Verify\u003c/b>\u003csmall>run or check\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Judge\u003c/b>\u003csmall>rubric\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Deduplicate\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Decontaminate\u003c/b>\u003csmall>against eval\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Mix with real data\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Validate\u003c/b> format: valid JSON, required fields, sensible length, the right language.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Verify\u003c/b> wherever code can: execute generated SQL or code, check that extracted values appear in the source text, check that the label matches a rule.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Judge\u003c/b> the rest with a rubric and an LLM judge that you have checked against a few dozen human labels; drop low scores.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Deduplicate\u003c/b> exact and near-duplicates.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Decontaminate:\u003c/b> remove any training example that shares a long word sequence (8 to 13 words is a common threshold) with an eval item, so your scores stay honest.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Read 50 random survivors\u003c/b> and check the category balance and length distribution. It is normal to discard a large share of what you generated.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>A dependency-free near-duplicate filter using word shingles and Jaccard similarity:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def shingles(text, n=5):\n    w = text.lower().split()\n    return {\" \".join(w[i:i + n]) for i in range(max(1, len(w) - n + 1))}\n\ndef drop_near_duplicates(texts, threshold=0.7):\n    kept, sigs = [], []\n    for t in texts:\n        s = shingles(t)\n        if all(len(s &amp; k) / len(s | k) &lt; threshold for k in sigs):\n            kept.append(t)\n            sigs.append(s)\n    return kept\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>This compares every pair, which is fine for a few thousand items. For millions, use MinHash with locality-sensitive hashing (the \u003ccode>datasketch\u003c/code> library) or embeddings with a nearest-neighbour index.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Risks\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Model collapse.\u003c/b> Shumailov et al. showed that training generation after generation on model-generated data makes the rare \"tails\" of the original distribution disappear. Keep real data in the mix, prefer verified synthetic data, and do not train repeatedly on your own model's unfiltered outputs.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Inherited errors and bias.\u003c/b> The generator's mistakes and blind spots become your model's.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Homogeneous style.\u003c/b> Synthetic text often sounds alike; real user messages are messier. Your eval set should stay real.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Terms.\u003c/b> The same output-use terms apply as for distillation.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Tools: plain Python and an API go a long way; \u003cb>distilabel\u003c/b>, NVIDIA's \u003cb>NeMo Data Designer\u003c/b> and Unsloth's \u003cb>Data Recipes\u003c/b> package these pipelines.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you generate, deduplicate and filter synthetic examples?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Generate from an attribute grid or rotating seed examples at high temperature so items differ by construction; validate format; verify by execution or source checks where possible; score the rest with a validated rubric judge and drop low scores; remove exact duplicates by hash and near-duplicates by shingle Jaccard, MinHash or embedding similarity; remove anything overlapping the eval set; read a random sample and check balance; then mix the survivors with real data.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does asking a model for 1,000 examples in one go produce poor data, and how do you fix it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The model gravitates to a few typical cases, so you get near-copies with surface changes. Fix it by generating one example per combination of controlled attributes, rotating seeds, and expanding a list of distinct scenarios one by one.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is decontamination, and what happens if you skip it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Removing training items that overlap the eval set. Without it, the model may have seen eval questions or close paraphrases during training, so eval scores overstate real performance.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is model collapse, and what is the practical defence?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Repeatedly training on model-generated data loses the rare parts of the real distribution and degrades models over generations. Keep real human data in every training mix and only add synthetic data that passed verification.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:evaluating-fine-tunes-and-catastrophic-forgettin":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A fine-tune can win on its task and quietly lose elsewhere: general reasoning, instruction following, safety refusals, even formatting. \u003cb>Catastrophic forgetting\u003c/b> is the name for losing earlier abilities after training on something new. Evaluate in layers: the task against the best prompted baseline, a general-capability regression suite, a safety suite and operational checks.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Four layers of evaluation\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Layer\u003c/th>\u003cth>Question\u003c/th>\u003cth>What to run\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Task\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Did we win on the job?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Held-out real examples; exact match, F1, pass rate or judge score; base model, fine-tune and best prompted frontier model side by side\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>General capability\u003c/td>\u003ctd>What did we lose?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Small slices of public benchmarks (for example MMLU-Pro, GSM8K, IFEval) via lm-evaluation-harness or lighteval, plus your own \"other tasks\" set\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Safety and behaviour\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Did we break guardrails?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Harmful-request refusals, over-refusal of benign requests, prompt-injection cases, personal-data leakage, tone\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Operational\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Is it shippable?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Latency at p50 and p95, answer length, stopping correctly, JSON validity, cost per 1,000 requests\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>How much general capability matters depends on use. A classifier hidden behind a router can afford small losses on maths; a general assistant cannot. Measure either way, because real inputs drift outside the task.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What the research says\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>LoRA learns less and forgets less\u003c/b> (Biderman et al., 2024): in code and maths, standard low-rank LoRA gained less on the target domain than full fine-tuning, but better preserved performance outside it. Parameter-efficient methods are a forgetting defence, not only a memory saver.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Fine-tuning erodes safety\u003c/b> (Qi et al., 2023): ten adversarial examples, costing under $0.20 through a fine-tuning API, made GPT-3.5 Turbo answer nearly any harmful request. Fine-tuning on ordinary benign datasets also degraded safety, to a lesser extent. Re-run safety evals after every fine-tune, however innocent the data.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Reading a comparison\u003c/h4>\u003cp>An illustrative result for a ticket-routing fine-tune:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>\u003c/th>\u003cth>Base small model\u003c/th>\u003cth>Fine-tune\u003c/th>\u003cth>Prompted frontier\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Task exact match\u003c/td>\u003ctd>61%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>93%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>95%\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Instruction-following suite\u003c/td>\u003ctd>78%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>74%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>90%\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Refuses harmful requests\u003c/td>\u003ctd>97%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>89%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>99%\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>p95 latency\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.4 s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.4 s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2.1 s\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Cost per 1,000 requests\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$0.20\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$0.20\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$7.50\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The task win is large and near the frontier model at a fraction of the cost. The 4-point instruction-following loss is acceptable for a router. The 8-point safety drop is not: add refusal examples to the training mix and re-test before shipping.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Reducing forgetting\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Mix in general data:\u003c/b> a slice of general instruction or chat data alongside the task data, plus examples of the refusals you want to keep.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Train gently:\u003c/b> LoRA with modest rank, fewer epochs, lower learning rate, and early stopping on a combined score rather than task loss alone.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Interpolate weights:\u003c/b> blending the fine-tuned weights back towards the base trades some task gain for retained general ability.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Narrow the job:\u003c/b> let the fine-tune handle only its task and route everything else to a general model.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Eval hygiene\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Freeze and version eval sets; decontaminate training data against them.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use the same decoding settings for every candidate (temperature, max tokens, prompt).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Mind the noise. With 200 eval items, a 90% accuracy has a standard error of about 2.1 points, so a 95% interval is roughly ±4 points. Differences smaller than that need more items or a paired analysis (the Evals module covers this).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Read outputs, not only scores.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Tools: \u003cb>lm-evaluation-harness\u003c/b> (EleutherAI) and \u003cb>lighteval\u003c/b> (Hugging Face) run standard benchmarks against local or served models; \u003cb>Inspect\u003c/b> and \u003cb>promptfoo\u003c/b> suit task and safety suites.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you check a fine-tune's task gains and its general-capability losses?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Run the same frozen, decontaminated evals with identical decoding on the base model, the fine-tune and the best prompted frontier model: the held-out task set for gains; small slices of general benchmarks such as MMLU-Pro, GSM8K and IFEval plus your own other-task set for losses; a safety suite for refusals, over-refusals and injection; and operational checks for latency, length, stopping and cost. Compare with confidence intervals and read samples; if losses matter, mix general and safety data into training, train more gently, or narrow the model's role.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your fine-tune scores 88% and the baseline 85% on the same 200 items. Is that a real win?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Not clearly. Each score has a standard error of about 2.3 to 2.5 points, so the difference of 3 points has a standard error of about 3.4 and a 95% interval of roughly 3 ± 6.7 points, which includes zero. Use more items or a paired comparison on the same items.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">You fine-tuned on harmless customer-support data. Why re-run safety evals?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Research found that even benign fine-tuning can weaken a model's safety behaviour, and fine-tuning can also shift refusals, tone and injection resistance. Only measuring tells you whether this fine-tune is affected.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:fine-tuning-for-function-calling":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A small model can become a reliable \u003cb>tool caller\u003c/b> for your own tools if you fine-tune it on verified examples of when to call which tool with what arguments, and when to call nothing. Train in exactly the format your serving stack parses, include the negative cases, and measure tool choice and arguments separately.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why fine-tune for tools\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Routers and small agents\u003c/b> make many tool decisions per task; a fine-tuned small model cuts latency and cost on every step.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>On-device and private assistants\u003c/b> cannot call a frontier model.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Your tools are unusual:\u003c/b> dozens of similar internal APIs, odd enums, strict id formats that general models fumble.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Two results show the potential. Gorilla (2023), a fine-tuned LLaMA model, surpassed GPT-4 at writing correct API calls for its benchmark. APIGen (2024) trained models on verified function-calling data and released 60,000 examples; its 7B model achieved state-of-the-art results on the Berkeley Function-Calling Benchmark at the time, ahead of several GPT-4 models, and its 1B model beat GPT-3.5 Turbo and Claude 3 Haiku.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The data format\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Training examples are conversations that include the assistant's tool calls, the tool results and the final answer, plus the list of tool schemas. This is TRL's format:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>example = {\n  \"messages\": [\n    {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Where is order 8812?\"},\n    {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"tool_calls\": [{\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\n        \"name\": \"get_order\", \"arguments\": {\"order_id\": \"8812\"}}}]},\n    {\"role\": \"tool\", \"name\": \"get_order\", \"content\": \"{\\\"status\\\": \\\"shipped\\\", \\\"eta\\\": \\\"2026-11-02\\\"}\"},\n    {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": \"Order 8812 has shipped and should arrive on 2 November.\"},\n  ],\n  \"tools\": [get_order_schema, search_orders_schema, create_ticket_schema],\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The model's chat template renders the tool schemas into the prompt and the call into model-specific markup (many open models wrap it in tags such as \u003ccode>&lt;tool_call&gt;...&lt;/tool_call&gt;\u003c/code>). Use the base model's \u003cb>native\u003c/b> tool template. Your server must parse the same markup: vLLM, for example, needs \u003ccode>--enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser &lt;parser&gt;\u003c/code> set to the parser for that model family. If training and serving formats differ, calls arrive as plain text and nothing executes. Tool results are inputs, so train only on assistant turns.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What the dataset must cover\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Case\u003c/th>\u003cth>Example\u003c/th>\u003cth>Teaches\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Single call\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"Where is order 8812?\" → \u003ccode>get_order\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The basic mapping\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Similar tools\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>get_order\u003c/code> versus \u003ccode>search_orders\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Reading the schemas\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Argument normalisation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"Friday next week\" → \u003ccode>2026-11-06\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Correct, typed arguments\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>No tool needed\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"Thanks, that's all\" → plain reply\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Not over-calling\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Missing information\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"Refund my order\" with no id → ask for it\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Not inventing arguments\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Several calls\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"Compare orders 8812 and 8813\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Parallel or sequential calls\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Using the result\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tool output → grounded final answer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Closing the loop\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Tool error\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tool returns an error → retry or explain\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Recovery\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Shuffle the order of tools in the schema list and add distractor tools, so the model learns to read schemas rather than memorise positions.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Generating verified data\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The APIGen recipe applies to your own tools:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Generate\u003c/b> requests from an attribute grid, or take real traffic and record a frontier model calling your tools (distillation).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Format check:\u003c/b> valid JSON, the function exists, arguments match the schema types and enums.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Execution check:\u003c/b> run the call against the real API or a sandbox; it must succeed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Semantic check:\u003c/b> a judge confirms the call and its result actually answer the request.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Only examples that pass all three go into training.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Evaluating tool calling\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Score these separately on a held-out set:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Tool selection accuracy:\u003c/b> right function, or correctly no function.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Argument accuracy:\u003c/b> exact match after normalisation, or structural (AST) matching as the Berkeley Function-Calling Leaderboard does.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Irrelevance handling:\u003c/b> no call when none is needed; a question when information is missing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>End-to-end task success\u003c/b> in a sandboxed environment for multi-step tasks.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Latency and cost\u003c/b> per task.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Run the frontier model through the same eval. Public leaderboards such as BFCL help you pick a base model; your own eval decides whether the fine-tune ships.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Training\u003c/h4>\u003cp>SFT with LoRA on the messages-plus-tools dataset is the standard first step. For multi-step agents, a GRPO stage can follow, with the reward set to task success in a simulated environment; TRL's GRPO trainer can run tool-using rollouts. Keep a frontier model as fallback for requests the small model flags as uncertain.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you fine-tune a small model to emit tool calls?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Pick a base model with a native tool-calling template; build conversations with the user request, assistant \u003ccode>tool_calls\u003c/code>, tool results and the final answer, plus a \u003ccode>tools\u003c/code> column of JSON schemas, covering single and multiple calls, similar tools, no-call and missing-information cases and tool errors, with shuffled and distractor tools; verify every example by format, execution and semantic checks; train with LoRA SFT on assistant turns only; serve with the matching tool-call parser; and evaluate tool selection, argument accuracy, irrelevance handling and end-to-end success against a frontier baseline.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why include examples where no tool should be called?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Without them the model learns that every request needs a call, so it calls tools on greetings or invents arguments. Negative examples teach it to answer directly or ask for missing information.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">The fine-tune looks perfect in a notebook, but in production its tool calls show up as plain text. What is the likely cause?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The serving stack does not parse the markup the model was trained to produce, because the chat template or the server's tool-call parser does not match the training format.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:finetune/where-to-fine-tune-unsloth-axolotl-trl-and-hoste":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>You can fine-tune open models yourself with \u003cb>TRL\u003c/b> (the reference library), \u003cb>Unsloth\u003c/b> (faster and leaner on one GPU) or \u003cb>Axolotl\u003c/b> (YAML configs, multi-GPU), or hand data to a \u003cb>hosted service\u003c/b>. Self-hosted tools give you the weights and full control; hosted services save setup but tie the result to their platform. As of late 2026, the hosted landscape is shifting, so portability matters.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The self-hosted libraries\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Tool\u003c/th>\u003cth>What it is\u003c/th>\u003cth>Best for\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>TRL (Hugging Face)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Trainers for SFT, DPO, GRPO, KTO, reward modelling; built on Transformers and PEFT; a \u003ccode>trl sft\u003c/code> CLI\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Learning, custom training loops, research methods\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Unsloth\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Optimised kernels and a wrapper around TRL; desktop app and Studio UI; GGUF export\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fast, low-memory runs on one GPU, notebooks, local models\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Axolotl\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Config-driven training on top of the same stack; multi-GPU with DeepSpeed or FSDP\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Repeatable runs, larger models, teams that prefer configs to code\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Tinker (Thinking Machines)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A hosted API exposing the training loop itself (forward-backward, optimiser step, sampling) with LoRA\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Custom RL and research without managing GPUs\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Unsloth wraps the same TRL trainers, so code moves between them easily:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from unsloth import FastLanguageModel\n\nmodel, tokenizer = FastLanguageModel.from_pretrained(\n    model_name=\"&lt;small-model&gt;\", max_seq_length=2048, load_in_4bit=True)\nmodel = FastLanguageModel.get_peft_model(\n    model, r=16, lora_alpha=16, lora_dropout=0,\n    target_modules=[\"q_proj\", \"k_proj\", \"v_proj\", \"o_proj\", \"gate_proj\", \"up_proj\", \"down_proj\"],\n    use_gradient_checkpointing=\"unsloth\", random_state=3407)\n# ... train with trl.SFTTrainer exactly as in the SFT lesson ...\nmodel.save_pretrained_merged(\"out/merged\", tokenizer, save_method=\"merged_16bit\")   # for vLLM\nmodel.save_pretrained_gguf(\"out/gguf\", tokenizer, quantization_method=\"q4_k_m\")      # for llama.cpp, Ollama\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The same run as an Axolotl config, launched with \u003ccode>axolotl train config.yml\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>base_model: &lt;small-model&gt;\nload_in_4bit: true\nadapter: qlora\nlora_r: 16\nlora_alpha: 32\nlora_target_linear: true\ndatasets:\n  - path: data/train.jsonl\n    ds_type: json\n    type: chat_template\n    field_messages: messages\n    roles_to_train: [\"assistant\"]\nval_set_size: 0.05\nsequence_len: 2048\nsample_packing: true\nmicro_batch_size: 2\ngradient_accumulation_steps: 8\nnum_epochs: 2\nlearning_rate: 0.0001\nbf16: auto\ngradient_checkpointing: true\noutput_dir: ./out/qlora\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Hosted fine-tuning, as of late 2026\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Service\u003c/th>\u003cth>Offers\u003c/th>\u003cth>Watch for\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>OpenAI\u003c/td>\u003ctd>SFT, DPO, reinforcement fine-tuning on selected GPT models\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Being wound down: closed to new organisations since May 2026, no new jobs for anyone after 6 January 2027; fine-tunes run until their base model is deprecated\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Microsoft Foundry\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Supervised fine-tuning of selected models, deployed in your Azure tenant\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Model and region availability\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Google (Vertex AI, now under Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Supervised, preference and RL tuning for Gemini models; tuning for open models\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Gemini tunes stay on Google's platform\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>AWS Bedrock\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Supervised fine-tuning, distillation and reinforcement fine-tuning for selected models\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Provisioned capacity for serving custom models\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Together AI, Fireworks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>LoRA and full fine-tuning of open models, preference tuning, RL (GRPO or RFT); hosted LoRA serving\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Check you can download the weights\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Rules of thumb:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Data cannot leave your network, or you need the weights:\u003c/b> self-host with Unsloth, Axolotl or TRL on your own or rented GPUs.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>No ML infrastructure and a closed model already works:\u003c/b> a cloud provider's tuning service, accepting lock-in.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Open model, no GPUs to manage:\u003c/b> an open-model platform, preferably one that lets you export the adapter.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Custom RL loops:\u003c/b> TRL or Unsloth on your GPUs, or Tinker.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Getting the result into production\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Adapter\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Merge into 16-bit base\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Quantise\u003c/b>\u003csmall>GGUF, AWQ, FP8\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Serve\u003c/b>\u003csmall>vLLM, SGLang, llama.cpp\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Or skip merging and serve adapters directly with vLLM's multi-LoRA support, which lets one GPU serve many task or customer adapters on a shared base. Either way, store the base model id and revision, the adapter, the training data version and the eval report together, so you can rebuild or roll back. Serving details are in the Serving module.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Lock-in.\u003c/b> A hosted fine-tune of a closed model cannot move, and it disappears when the provider retires the base model. Keep the training data and evals as the portable asset.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Version drift.\u003c/b> Unsloth, TRL, Transformers and PEFT move fast; pin versions in \u003ccode>requirements.txt\u003c/code> and record them with each run.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Free notebook GPUs\u003c/b> (often a 16 GB T4) suit 1B to 8B QLoRA runs but lack bf16, so use fp16 there.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your company must keep customer data in its own cloud account and wants to serve the model on its own GPUs. Which fine-tuning route fits?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Self-hosted training with Unsloth, Axolotl or TRL on GPUs inside that account, producing open weights that you merge, quantise and serve with vLLM or SGLang. Hosted services would move data or weights outside your control.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does the OpenAI fine-tuning wind-down teach about hosted fine-tunes in general?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A hosted fine-tune of a closed model lives only as long as the provider supports its platform and base model. Treat your curated data, eval suite and training recipe as the durable assets, and prefer setups where you can export or retrain the weights elsewhere.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">When would you pick Axolotl over writing a TRL script?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When you want repeatable, config-driven runs that teammates can review and rerun, especially for larger models across several GPUs with DeepSpeed or FSDP, without maintaining training code.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","lab:finetune/qlora-in-a-notebook":"\u003cp>You will teach a small model a narrow job, turning messy shop order notes into strict JSON, and measure it before and after training on the same held-out set. The task is synthetic so the lab is self-contained and gradable by exact match.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A notebook with a GPU of 12 GB or more: Google Colab or Kaggle free tiers (usually a 16 GB T4) work.\u003c/li>\u003cli>About 10 minutes of training and 10 minutes of evaluation.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Install the current stack (TRL 1.x, Transformers 5):\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>pip install -U trl peft bitsandbytes datasets accelerate\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Generate 1,200 training and 200 test examples. Each note is unique; the target follows fixed conventions (singular item names, integer quantity, sizes S to XL or null, a boolean for gift wrap).\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import json, random\nrng = random.Random(0)\nSYSTEM = ('Extract the order as JSON with keys \"item\" (one of: mug, t-shirt, hoodie, notebook, '\n          'water bottle, cap, tote bag, poster), \"quantity\" (integer), \"size\" (\"S\", \"M\", \"L\", \"XL\" '\n          'or null) and \"gift_wrap\" (true or false). Reply with JSON only.')\nITEMS = [\"mug\", \"t-shirt\", \"hoodie\", \"notebook\", \"water bottle\", \"cap\", \"tote bag\", \"poster\"]\nSIZED = {\"t-shirt\", \"hoodie\", \"cap\"}\nSIZE_WORDS = {\"S\": [\"small\", \"S\", \"size s\"], \"M\": [\"medium\", \"M\", \"size m\"],\n              \"L\": [\"large\", \"L\", \"size l\"], \"XL\": [\"extra large\", \"XL\", \"size xl\"]}\nNUM_WORDS = [\"one\", \"two\", \"three\", \"four\", \"five\", \"six\"]\nTEMPLATES = [\"hi, could I get {qty} {item} in {size}? {gift}\", \"{qty} x {item} ({size}) pls. {gift}\",\n             \"Order: {item}, qty {qty}, size {size}. {gift}\",\n             \"need {qty} of the {item}, {size} if you have it. {gift}\", \"{gift} {qty} {item}, {size}.\"]\nGIFT = {True: [\"It's a gift, please wrap it.\", \"gift wrap please\", \"Can you gift-wrap?\"],\n        False: [\"\", \"No wrapping needed.\", \"thanks!\"]}\n\ndef make():\n    item, qty, gift = rng.choice(ITEMS), rng.randint(1, 6), rng.random() &lt; 0.4\n    size = rng.choice(list(SIZE_WORDS)) if item in SIZED else None\n    note = rng.choice(TEMPLATES).format(\n        qty=rng.choice([str(qty), NUM_WORDS[qty - 1]]), item=item + (\"s\" if qty &gt; 1 else \"\"),\n        size=rng.choice(SIZE_WORDS[size]) if size else \"one size\", gift=rng.choice(GIFT[gift]))\n    target = {\"item\": item, \"quantity\": qty, \"size\": size, \"gift_wrap\": gift}\n    return {\"prompt\": [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": SYSTEM},\n                       {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \" \".join(note.split())}],\n            \"completion\": [{\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": json.dumps(target)}]}\n\nseen, rows = set(), []\nwhile len(rows) &lt; 1400:\n    r = make()\n    if r[\"prompt\"][1][\"content\"] not in seen:\n        seen.add(r[\"prompt\"][1][\"content\"])\n        rows.append(r)\ntrain, test = rows[:1200], rows[1200:]\nprint(train[0])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"2\">\u003cli>Load the model in 4-bit and write the scoring function. The code picks bf16 where the GPU supports it and fp16 on a T4.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import torch\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer, BitsAndBytesConfig\n\nMODEL = \"Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct\"     # any 1B to 4B instruct model with a chat template\nbf16 = torch.cuda.is_bf16_supported()\ndtype = torch.bfloat16 if bf16 else torch.float16\ntokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(MODEL)\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\n    MODEL, dtype=dtype, device_map=\"auto\",\n    quantization_config=BitsAndBytesConfig(load_in_4bit=True, bnb_4bit_quant_type=\"nf4\",\n                                           bnb_4bit_use_double_quant=True, bnb_4bit_compute_dtype=dtype))\n\ndef predict(m, rows, bs=16):\n    tokenizer.padding_side = \"left\"\n    outs = []\n    for i in range(0, len(rows), bs):\n        texts = [tokenizer.apply_chat_template(r[\"prompt\"], tokenize=False, add_generation_prompt=True)\n                 for r in rows[i:i + bs]]\n        enc = tokenizer(texts, return_tensors=\"pt\", padding=True).to(m.device)\n        gen = m.generate(**enc, max_new_tokens=60, do_sample=False)\n        outs += tokenizer.batch_decode(gen[:, enc[\"input_ids\"].shape[1]:], skip_special_tokens=True)\n    return outs\n\ndef parse(text):\n    text = text.strip().removeprefix(\"```json\").removesuffix(\"```\").strip()\n    try:\n        return json.loads(text)\n    except json.JSONDecodeError:\n        return None\n\ndef score(m, rows):\n    preds = predict(m, rows)\n    hits = [parse(p) == json.loads(r[\"completion\"][0][\"content\"]) for p, r in zip(preds, rows)]\n    return sum(hits) / len(rows), preds\n\nbase_acc, base_preds = score(model, test)\nprint(\"base exact match:\", base_acc)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"3\">\u003cli>Read ten of the base model's wrong answers. Note which conventions it misses (plural item names, \"medium\" instead of \"M\", \"four\" instead of 4, a size for a mug).\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003col start=\"4\">\u003cli>Train a LoRA adapter on the 4-bit model. 1,200 examples with an effective batch of 16 for 2 epochs is 150 steps.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from datasets import Dataset\nfrom peft import LoraConfig\nfrom trl import SFTConfig, SFTTrainer\n\ntokenizer.padding_side = \"right\"\nargs = SFTConfig(output_dir=\"out/orders-qlora\", num_train_epochs=2, per_device_train_batch_size=8,\n                 gradient_accumulation_steps=2, learning_rate=2e-4, lr_scheduler_type=\"cosine\",\n                 warmup_steps=0.05, max_length=512, logging_steps=10, bf16=bf16, fp16=not bf16)\ntrainer = SFTTrainer(model=model, args=args, processing_class=tokenizer,\n                     train_dataset=Dataset.from_list(train),\n                     peft_config=LoraConfig(r=16, lora_alpha=32, target_modules=\"all-linear\",\n                                            task_type=\"CAUSAL_LM\"))\ntrainer.train()\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"5\">\u003cli>Score the fine-tuned model on the same 200 test notes and print the remaining failures.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>tuned = trainer.model.eval()\ntuned.config.use_cache = True\ntuned_acc, tuned_preds = score(tuned, test)\nprint(\"fine-tuned exact match:\", tuned_acc)\nfor p, r in zip(tuned_preds, test):\n    if parse(p) != json.loads(r[\"completion\"][0][\"content\"]):\n        print(r[\"prompt\"][1][\"content\"], \"|\", p)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"6\">\u003cli>Write ten messy notes yourself, in styles the templates never used (\"2 large hoodies + wrap them pls\", \"a mug. no, make that three\"), with the correct JSON. Score both models on them with \u003ccode>score\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003col start=\"7\">\u003cli>Save the adapter with \u003ccode>trainer.save_model(\"out/orders-adapter\")\u003c/code> and check its size on disk.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>The base model usually returns plausible JSON but breaks the conventions, so its exact-match score is modest.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Training loss drops steeply in the first few dozen steps and then flattens; the whole run takes minutes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The fine-tune scores far higher on the templated test set, often close to perfect.\u003c/li>\u003cli>On your hand-written notes the gap is smaller and new failures appear. The test set shares templates with the training set, so it overstates generalisation: the same lesson as splitting real data by user or time.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The adapter holds about 18.5M parameters (r = 16 on all linear layers of this 1.5B model), roughly 37 MB in 16-bit or 74 MB if saved in 32-bit, against about 3 GB for the full model in 16-bit.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is the fine-tune's score on the templated test set an optimistic estimate?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The test notes come from the same templates and vocabulary as the training notes, so they are near-duplicates in structure. Real users write differently, which is why the hand-written set, and in real projects a test set split by user or time, gives the honest number.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What would you change first if the fine-tuned model still wrote sizes for mugs?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Check the training data contains enough unsized items with \u003ccode>\"size\": null\u003c/code> and that the targets are consistent, then add targeted examples. Changing data is usually more effective than changing hyperparameters.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","b:small-model-that-beats-a-big-one":"\u003ch4>The brief\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Pick one narrow, high-volume task from your work or from Atlas, and show with evidence that a fine-tuned open model of 8B parameters or fewer matches or beats a prompted frontier model on it, at a fraction of the cost and latency. Good candidates: ticket classification, field extraction, text-to-SQL over a known schema, or, as the optional Atlas extension, distilling Atlas's router (from M9) that decides whether to retrieve, answer or act. Starting point for the Atlas route: Atlas after M14, with traces from its router.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Requirements\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A held-out eval set of at least 150 real or realistic examples with automatic scoring, split by user or time\u003c/li>\u003cli>Two baselines: the best prompted frontier model, and the prompted small base model\u003c/li>\u003cli>At least 1,000 training examples from traces, teacher distillation or filtered synthetic data, deduplicated and decontaminated against the eval set, with a data card\u003c/li>\u003cli>A LoRA or QLoRA SFT run, plus one attempted DPO or GRPO stage that you measure honestly, even if it does not help\u003c/li>\u003cli>Regression checks: a general-capability slice and a 50-prompt safety set, run on base and fine-tune\u003c/li>\u003cli>The fine-tune served behind an OpenAI-compatible endpoint (vLLM, SGLang or llama.cpp) with measured p50 and p95 latency and cost per 1,000 requests\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Milestones\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Define the task and success metric in three sentences; build and freeze the eval set.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Run both baselines through one evaluation script; record quality, latency and cost.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Build the training set: run the frontier model with its best prompt over a few thousand prompts, filter with validators or a rubric judge, deduplicate, decontaminate, and write the data card.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Train two or three SFT configurations (for example r = 16 versus 32, one versus two epochs) and keep the best by eval score, not training loss.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add a DPO stage (pairs from the SFT model's failures and corrections) or a GRPO stage (if a verifier exists); compare against SFT alone.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Run the regression and safety checks and fix any serious drop by changing the data mix.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Serve the winner, load-test it, and write a one-page report with a recommendation and a cascade design (small model first, frontier fallback on low confidence).\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Done when\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>python eval.py --model &lt;name&gt;\u003c/code> scores any candidate on the frozen eval set\u003c/li>\u003cli>The report shows base, fine-tune and frontier side by side for quality, p95 latency, cost per 1,000 requests and regression results, with at least five analysed failure cases\u003c/li>\u003cli>The fine-tune is within the confidence interval of the frontier model or better on the task metric, or the report explains convincingly why it is not and what would close the gap\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Stretch goals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Plot a learning curve: eval score against 250, 500, 1,000 and all training examples\u003c/li>\u003cli>Try a 1B to 3B student and find the smallest model that clears the bar\u003c/li>\u003cli>Implement the cascade and measure the blended quality and cost on a day of replayed traffic\u003c/li>\u003cli>Serve several task adapters on one base model with vLLM's multi-LoRA support\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Hints\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Most of the gain comes from data quality; spend your time on filtering and failure cases, not hyperparameter sweeps.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If the student never stops or emits stray tags, compare the training chat template with the serving template first.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Keep the frontier model as a fallback in production; a small model that is right 97% of the time and escalates the rest is usually the winning design.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>"}]);
